  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 325 AM EST Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread moderate to locally heavy rainfall \nis expected today, with a few thunderstorms possible.\n\nGOES water vapor channels depict a potent upper low centered \nover the Great Lakes with the trailing axis back into the \nCentral Plains. Shortwave energy is evident from WV into PA. \nThere is essentially a series of cold fronts associated with the\nupper trough, with the leading boundary in vicinity of the Blue\nRidge early this morning. A line of showers/tstms has developed\nacross central VA well in advance of the shortwave and ahead of\nthe initial cold front where there is a plume of 1000+ J/kg of \nMUCAPE. This line of showers/tstms is quickly tracking to the \nNE. Brief heavy rain and occasional cloud to ground lightning \nare the main hazards. Otherwise, mostly cloudy and quite mild \nfor early November with temperatures in the mid 60s to around \n70F.\n\nThe initial cold front will be slow to push E through midday, \nbefore moving E toward the coast later this afternoon and \nevening as the parent upper low pushes SE. The airmass ahead of \nthe trough and leading cold front is very moist for early \nNovember with PW values of 200-250% of normal. Forcing for \nascent will increase along the boundary as it pushes E this \nafternoon with moderate to locally heavy rain possible toward \nthe coast, with the best signal from NE NC into Hampton Roads. \nThe 06/00z REFS generally depicts QPF of 0.5- 1.0\" from the \nPiedmont through the RIC metro, increasing to 1.0-1.5\" for the \nEastern Shore, and 1.5-2.5\" from NE NC into Hampton Roads with \nhigher amounts possible. Additionally, the REFS has a contour of\na 30% chc of 3\"/3hr from NE NC to Hampton Roads from 18-21z \nthis afternoon, with some moderate rain potentially lingering \ninto the evening. WPC has maintained a Marginal ERO for the \neastern half of the area. The greatest flood threat will be in \nurban locations as the flood threat will be limited in rural \nareas due to ongoing drought and low streamflow. A similar \nportion of the CWA is covered by a Marginal Severe Risk, mainly \nfor 5% wind, with a 2% tornado threat in SE VA and NE NC. Bulk- \nShear will generally remain between 35-45kt with MUCAPE waning \nthrough the day but remaining 500-750J/kg in SE portions of the \narea, and locally up to 1000J/kg in interior NE NC. These \ningredients together could allow for a brief strong wind gusts \nwith any stronger tstm embedded within the broader line of \nshowers. There is also a very small tornado threat due to the \nweak 0-1 SRH (~100m^2/s^2), however, confidence remains low at \nthis time, especially given that low-level lapse rates look \nrather poor in forecast soundings.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drying out and becoming seasonably cool this \nweekend into early next week.\n\nThe closed upper low will drop to the WSW from the Eastern \nGreat Lake into the northern Mid-Atlantic/southern New England \nregion on Saturday, reinforcing northwesterly flow locally. Much\ncooler and drier air is forecasted to advect into the area, \nwith high temperatures settling back to seasonal averages. High \ntemperatures this weekend are expected to be in the lower to mid\n60s and into the mid to upper 60s by early next week, and \npotentially reaching ~70F Tuesday. Breezy Saturday as wind gusts\nwill be around 20-25mph out of the NW. Overnight/early morning \nlows drop into the mid/upper 40s W and N early Saturday morning,\nwith mid 50s SE. Lows will then generally be in the mid 30s to \nlower 40s later this weekend into early next week. The recent \nensemble guidance is continuing to show dry weather persisting \ninto the middle of next week as opposed to bringing in any more \nrain chances.",
